lessons.notes:
## Reading input with Scanner

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int a = sc.nextInt();
        int b = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.println(a + b);
    }
}
```

`nextInt()` skips any whitespace, so numbers can be on one line or on separate lines.

## Methods

| Method | Reads |
|--------|-------|
| `nextInt()` | an `int` |
| `nextLong()` | a `long` |
| `nextDouble()` | a `double` |
| `next()` | one word |
| `nextLine()` | the rest of the current line |
| `hasNext()` / `hasNextInt()` | whether more input exists |

## Mixing nextInt and nextLine

```java
int n = sc.nextInt();
sc.nextLine();                 // consume the leftover newline
String sentence = sc.nextLine();
```

## Formatted output

```java
System.out.printf("%d items%n", 3);
System.out.printf("%.2f%n", 2.0 / 3);   // 0.67
System.out.printf("%-6s|%4d|%n", "ab", 7);
String msg = String.format("%s is %d", "Ana", 15);
```

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `%d` | integer |
| `%f`, `%.2f` | decimal, with 2 digits |
| `%s` | string (or any object) |
| `%n` | platform newline |
| `%5d` | width 5, right-aligned |

## Faster input

For very large inputs, professionals use `BufferedReader`:

```java
BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
String line = br.readLine();
```

Scanner is simpler and perfectly fine for this course.

## Locale note

`printf` follows your computer's language settings, and some locales print `2,33` instead of `2.33`. Pass `Locale.US` to always get a dot: `System.out.printf(Locale.US, "%.2f%n", x);` (import `java.util.Locale`).

