lessons.notes:
## if / else if / else

```java
int n = sc.nextInt();
if (n > 0) {
    System.out.println("positive");
} else if (n < 0) {
    System.out.println("negative");
} else {
    System.out.println("zero");
}
```

Braces are optional for a single statement, but always using them avoids bugs.

## Operators

| Operator | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `==`, `!=` | equal, not equal (for primitives) |
| `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` | ordering |
| `&&` | and (short-circuit) |
| `\|\|` | or (short-circuit) |
| `!` | not |

**Short-circuit** means Java stops early: in `a != 0 && b / a > 1`, the division never runs when `a` is 0.

## Strings

```java
"hi".equals(word)            // true if same text
word.equalsIgnoreCase("HI")  // ignores case
```

## Ternary operator

```java
String label = (n % 2 == 0) ? "even" : "odd";
```

## switch

Modern arrow syntax:

```java
switch (op) {
    case "+" -> System.out.println(a + b);
    case "-" -> System.out.println(a - b);
    default -> System.out.println("unknown");
}
```

As an expression:

```java
int days = switch (month) {
    case 2 -> 28;
    case 4, 6, 9, 11 -> 30;
    default -> 31;
};
```

The old style with `case X:` and `break;` still works, but forgetting `break` causes **fall-through**, a common bug. Prefer arrows.

