lessons.notes:
## Anatomy of a method

```java
static double average(int a, int b) {
    return (a + b) / 2.0;
}
```

- `static`: belongs to the class (no object needed). You will meet non-static methods with classes.
- `double`: the **return type**. Use `void` when nothing is returned.
- `average`: the name, in camelCase, usually a verb.
- `(int a, int b)`: parameters with types.

Every path through a non-void method must `return` a value of the right type.

## Calling methods

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    double avg = average(3, 4);    // 3.5
    System.out.println(avg);
}
```

## Overloading

```java
static int max(int a, int b) { return a > b ? a : b; }
static int max(int a, int b, int c) { return max(max(a, b), c); }
```

## Recursion

```java
static long factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;          // base case
    return n * factorial(n - 1);   // recursive case
}
```

Without a base case, recursion ends with `StackOverflowError`.

## Pass by value

```java
static void addOne(int x) { x++; }

int n = 5;
addOne(n);
System.out.println(n);   // still 5
```

Objects are passed by copying the **reference**, so a method can change an object's contents (you will see this with arrays).

## Good method design

- One method, one job.
- Short: if it does not fit on the screen, split it.
- Clear names: `isPrime`, `computeTotal`, `printReport`.

lessons.example:
public class Main {
    static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n < 2) return false;
        for (int d = 2; (long) d * d <= n; d++) {
            if (n % d == 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    static long factorial(int n) {
        return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * factorial(n - 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i < 30; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i)) System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("10! = " + factorial(10));
    }
}

