lessons.notes:
## Creating arrays

```java
int[] a = new int[5];            // [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]
double[] d = new double[3];      // [0.0, 0.0, 0.0]
String[] names = {"Ana", "Leo"}; // literal
boolean[] seen = new boolean[10];
```

Default values: `0` for numbers, `false` for booleans, `null` for objects.

## Reading an array from input

```java
int n = sc.nextInt();
int[] nums = new int[n];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    nums[i] = sc.nextInt();
}
```

## Common algorithms

```java
int sum = 0, max = nums[0];
for (int x : nums) {
    sum += x;
    if (x > max) max = x;
}
```

Reverse in place:

```java
for (int i = 0, j = nums.length - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {
    int t = nums[i]; nums[i] = nums[j]; nums[j] = t;
}
```

## java.util.Arrays

| Method | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `Arrays.sort(a)` | sort ascending |
| `Arrays.toString(a)` | `"[1, 2, 3]"` |
| `Arrays.fill(a, v)` | set every element |
| `Arrays.copyOf(a, n)` | copy with new length |
| `Arrays.equals(a, b)` | compare contents |
| `Arrays.stream(a).sum()` | quick sum |

## References

Arrays are objects. `int[] b = a;` makes `b` point to the **same** array. Changing `b[0]` changes `a[0]`. Use `a.clone()` or `Arrays.copyOf` for a real copy.

## Fixed size

Arrays cannot grow. When you need a list that grows, use `ArrayList` (coming soon).

lessons.example:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2};
        int sum = 0, max = nums[0];
        for (int x : nums) {
            sum += x;
            if (x > max) max = x;
        }
        System.out.println("Sum: " + sum + ", max: " + max);
        int[] sorted = nums.clone();
        Arrays.sort(sorted);
        System.out.println("Original: " + Arrays.toString(nums));
        System.out.println("Sorted:   " + Arrays.toString(sorted));
        int[][] table = new int[3][3];
        for (int r = 0; r < 3; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++)
                table[r][c] = (r + 1) * (c + 1);
        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(table));
    }
}

