lessons.notes:
## ArrayList

```java
import java.util.*;

List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>();
nums.add(3);
nums.add(1);
nums.add(0, 9);           // insert at index 0 -> [9, 3, 1]
nums.get(1);              // 3
nums.set(1, 4);           // [9, 4, 1]
nums.remove(Integer.valueOf(9));  // remove by value
nums.remove(0);           // remove by index
Collections.sort(nums);
```

Declare variables with the **interface** type `List` and create with `new ArrayList<>()`. The empty `<>` (diamond) lets Java infer the type.

## Looping

```java
for (int x : nums) System.out.println(x);
for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) System.out.println(i + ": " + nums.get(i));
```

## Wrapper classes

| Primitive | Wrapper |
|-----------|---------|
| `int` | `Integer` |
| `double` | `Double` |
| `char` | `Character` |
| `boolean` | `Boolean` |

Compare wrapper objects with `equals`, not `==`.

## HashMap

```java
Map<String, Integer> stock = new HashMap<>();
stock.put("apple", 3);
stock.put("apple", 5);           // replaces
stock.containsKey("pear");       // false
stock.getOrDefault("pear", 0);   // 0
stock.remove("apple");
```

