```

## Interface vs abstract class

| Interface | Abstract class |
|-----------|----------------|
| A class can implement many | A class extends only one |
| No instance fields (only constants) | Can have fields and constructors |
| Describes a **capability** | Shares **common code** |

## Important built-in interfaces

- `Comparable<T>`: natural ordering with `compareTo`.
- `Comparator<T>`: a separate ordering strategy.
- `Iterable<T>`: works with for-each.
- `Runnable`, `Function<T, R>`, `Predicate<T>`, `Supplier<T>`: functional interfaces.

## Comparable example

```java
record Student(String name, int score) implements Comparable<Student> {
    public int compareTo(Student o) {
